WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.500
SARSA.

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:06.733
Almost identical to Q-learning, but it learns to behave very differently near danger.

3
00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:13.900
SARSA stands for state, action, reward, next state, next action: the quintuple it updates from.

4
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.538
It is Q-learning's on-policy sibling.

5
00:00:16.538 --> 00:00:23.867
Instead of the best next action, it bootstraps from the action it will actually take, exploratory stumbles included.

6
00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:26.300
Here's the contrast.

7
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.618
Q-learning bootstraps from the best next action.

8
00:00:29.618 --> 00:00:32.133
SARSA uses the action it actually takes.

9
00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:41.733
So Q-learning learns about the greedy policy it isn't following, while SARSA learns about the policy it is really running, with its ten percent random lurches.

10
00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:43.923
Q-learning finds the optimum.

11
00:00:43.923 --> 00:00:46.333
SARSA respects the risk of exploring.

12
00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:49.500
The classic demo is cliff walking.

13
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.400
The shortest path runs right along the cliff edge.

14
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:59.067
Q-learning learns that optimal path, and keeps falling off during training, because its exploration stumbles.

15
00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:05.000
SARSA's values include the cost of its own stumbles, so it learns a detour with a safety margin.

16
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.033
In code, only the bootstrap target changes.

17
00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:11.900
Q-learning takes the max over next actions.

18
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.333
SARSA picks its next action first, then uses that action's value.

19
00:01:16.633 --> 00:01:19.667
It carries the chosen action forward, hence the name.

20
00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:24.167
Your rover grid has a crater rim along the shortest route.

21
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:28.900
If exploration happens on real or expensive hardware, caution matters.

22
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.133
With epsilon of zero point one, Q-learning loses about five percent of training rovers to the rim.

23
00:01:35.433 --> 00:01:37.100
SARSA loses almost none.

24
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:43.467
Interviewers ask this in disguise: your agent behaves recklessly during training, why?

25
00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:47.811
Often it is off-policy learning ignoring exploration risk.

26
00:01:47.811 --> 00:01:49.833
On-policy SARSA prices it in.

27
00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:51.807
Let's recap.

28
00:01:51.807 --> 00:01:54.900
SARSA bootstraps from the action actually taken.

29
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.333
It is on-policy, while Q-learning is off-policy.

30
00:01:58.633 --> 00:02:01.833
On a cliff, Q-learning is optimal and SARSA is safe.

31
00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:03.643
Neither is better.

32
00:02:03.643 --> 00:02:04.733
Next up: DQN.
